<capabilities>
<p>Massing 820,000 tons, the Agamemnon carries a weapons suite comparable to the Black Lion-class battlecruiser, built around naval autocannons of varying caliber as its primary anti-ship armament. The nose and broadside arcs mount NAC/30s and NAC/40s supplemented by heavy naval gauss rifles along the broadsides, giving the class devastating punch at medium engagement ranges. Twin NL/55 naval lasers in each arc provide sustained fire capability, while the aft quarter mounts a trio of lighter NAC/20s to discourage pursuit.</p><p>Where the Agamemnon distinguishes itself is its layered anti-fighter defense network. Each firing arc carries four extended-range PPCs, paired ER large lasers, and quad SRM-6 launchers fitted with Artemis IV fire control, creating overlapping fields of fire that punish aerospace strikes at every range bracket. Four anti-missile systems per arc and small pulse laser clusters provide the final defensive layer. An aerospace wing of eighteen fighters across two bays allows the Agamemnon to project its own fighter screen while defending against enemy strikes.</p><p>The class trades armor protection for speed, mounting only 735 tons of standard armor, significantly less than the smaller Sovetskii Soyuz. A lithium-fusion battery provides emergency jump capability, while an onboard mobile HPG ensures fleet communications independent of the HPG network. Four DropShip collars and over seventy thousand tons of cargo capacity give the class excellent independent operational endurance.</p>
</capabilities>

<overview>
<p>Named after the legendary Mycenaean king who commanded the Greek expedition to Troy, the Agamemnon-class heavy cruiser represented the Free Worlds League's boldest naval ambition since the fall of the Star League. Designed as the centerpiece of the FWLM's naval rearmament program, the class was intended to challenge the reactivated Star League-era WarShips emerging from hidden reserves and Clan mothball fleets. Falling between the venerable Black Lion and Sovetskii Soyuz classes in displacement, the Agamemnon was the first purpose-built heavy cruiser constructed in the Inner Sphere in centuries.</p><p>Built at the yards of Imstar Aerospace and Irian Naval Systems for the Free Worlds League Navy, the Agamemnon combined formidable firepower with the operational endurance necessary for extended independent patrols. Many naval analysts considered the class the most capable purpose-built combatant to emerge from a Successor State shipyard since the fall of the Star League, a reputation that only grew as the class entered active fleet service.</p>
</overview>

<deployment>
<p>The FWLS Menelaus completed shakedown trials along the Lyran border in early 3059 and quickly became the flagship of the League's resurgent navy. The only Agamemnon to remain under loyalist control during the Jihad, the Menelaus struck back in August 3070, operating alongside the Eagle-class frigates Galahad and Lancelot to ambush and destroy two Blakist-controlled WarShips in the Shiloh system. The veteran cruiser survived the war and was assigned to permanent station at Ionus, guarding the Illium Naval Yard as one of the Free Worlds League's last operational capital ships.</p><p>The FWLS Aineas entered service several months behind schedule after a complete electrical systems failure during acceptance trials allowed the corvette FWLS Tirana to embarrassingly "destroy" the larger vessel in exercises. Suborned by Word of Blake operatives before the Jihad, the Aineas appeared under Blakist colors at the Battle of Atreus in October 3068 and later joined the assault on Arc-Royal in 3072. The vessel was destroyed over Dieron in 3077.</p><p>The FWLS Hippolyta entered service in 3063 and was similarly suborned before hostilities began. The only compromised Agamemnon to survive the conflict, the Hippolyta was last observed in 3079 operating near Dayr Khuna with a Vincent-class corvette, raiding Duchy of Oriente shipping before vanishing entirely.</p><p>The FWLS Troy, whose construction benefited from Word of Blake technical assistance, entered service shortly before the Jihad. Also suborned, the Troy was destroyed alongside the Aegis-class Olympic during the assault on Arc-Royal in 3072.</p>
</deployment>

<history>
<p>The Agamemnon-class heavy cruiser emerged from the Free Worlds League's determination to rebuild its naval power in the wake of the Clan Invasion. While the Lyran Alliance and Federated Commonwealth invested in lighter escort designs, Captain-General Thomas Marik pursued an ambitious program to construct purpose-built capital ships capable of securing the League's borders and projecting power across its vast territory. The Agamemnon represented the apex of this strategy, a heavy cruiser that could serve as both fleet flagship and engagement anchor.</p><p>Design work leveraged recovered Star League technical data and the industrial expertise of the League's two premier naval shipyards. Imstar Aerospace on Atreus and Irian Technologies' naval subsidiary on Clipperton began parallel construction in the late 3050s, with the lead ship laid down at the Atreus yards. The FWLM's doctrine of aggressive fleet action over static defense shaped every aspect of the design, prioritizing offensive capability and independent operational range.</p><p>Commissioning proceeded on a staggered schedule. The first vessel entered service in late 3058, followed by a second in 3060 and a third in 3063. The fourth hull benefited from Word of Blake technical assistance that accelerated its construction timeline. By 3067, all four vessels were operational, giving the Free Worlds League the most formidable WarShip flotilla fielded by any single Successor State.</p><p>The Jihad shattered the class's promise within years. The Word of Blake's deep penetration of the League's military command structure allowed them to suborn three of the four Agamemnons. The extent of Blakist infiltration became brutally apparent in October 3068 when compromised vessels appeared under Word of Blake colors at the Battle of Atreus. By the end of the Jihad, three of the four hulls had been lost to various engagements or disappeared entirely, leaving only a single vessel under legitimate Free Worlds League authority.</p><p>That lone survivor left the Free Worlds League with one of only two operational WarShips in its entire navy. When the League was reconstituted in 3139, the class had been reduced to that solitary hull. The Agamemnon illustrates both the ambition and fragility of post-Star League shipbuilding: a design whose potential was never fully realized, its promise consumed by the treachery that claimed three-quarters of its production run before the vessels could prove their worth in legitimate service.</p>
</history>

